===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|honourable}}, from {{der|en|fro|honorable}}, {{m|fro|honurable}}, from {{der|en|la|honōrābilis}}, from {{m|la|honōrō||I honour}}; cognate with {{cog|it|onorabile}}, {{cog|es|honorable}}. {{surf|en|honor|-able}}. In this sense, largely displaced {{noncog|ang|ārfæst}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|ŏnʹərəbl|ŏnʹrəbl|a=RP}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈɒnəɹəbl̩/|/ˈɒnɹəbl̩/}}
* {{enPR|ŏnʹərəbl|ŏnʹrəbl|a=GA}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈɑnəɹəbl̩/|/ˈɑnɹəbl̩/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-honorable.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|hon|or|able}}, {{hyph|en|honor|able|nocaption=1}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}} {{tlb|en|American spelling}}

# Behaving in a manner that shows [[honor]]; [[decent]], having [[integrity]].
# [[worthy|Worthy]] of [[respect]]; [[respectable]].
#: {{syn|en|noble|venerable}}
#: {{ant|en|contemptible|despicable|mean}}
# Complying with cultural rules regarding [[honor]]; not provoking [[shame]] or [[disgrace]].
#: {{ux|en|That culture considered it no disgrace to be defeated in '''honorable''' combat.}}
# {{lb|en|politics}} {{n-g|A courtesy title, given in Britain and the Commonwealth to a [[cabinet minister]], [[minister of state]], or [[senator]], and in the United States to the president, vice president, congresspeople, state governors and legislators, and mayors.}}
#: {{syn|en|Hon'ble|q1=abbreviation|Hon.|q2=abbreviation}}

====Usage notes====
* Historically used in the United States (early 1900s) in mockery of Japanese and Chinese people (or {{m|en|'''honorable''' [[Jap]]s}}). [[Category:American English]] {{cln|en|ethnic slurs}}

